content:
Sara Gomez: Hey team — customer reports repeated SSE disconnects from Redwood hosted API when streaming long responses. They see partial outputs (last event truncated) and then the stream closes. Happens with both browser client and server-side clients behind a proxy. They attached a small repro and server logs.

Sara Gomez: Example client curl they used: `curl -N -H \"Accept: text/event-stream\" \\\"https://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream?model=text-gen-2\"` — returns a few events then connection drops.

Sara Gomez: Server logs snippet they pasted: 
```
2026-03-10T15:12:34.123Z INFO stream:start request_id=abc123 client_ip=1.2.3.4
2026-03-10T15:12:35.456 WARN stream:slow-consumer request_id=abc123 write_queue_bytes=524288
2026-03-10T15:12:36.789 ERROR stream:socket-write-failed request_id=abc123 err=EPIPE
2026-03-10T15:12:36.790 INFO stream:abort request_id=abc123 reason=slow_consumer_threshold
```

Sara Gomez: Customer also says billing showed token double-counting because client reconnected and got overlapping partial output. They're worried about cost spikes.

Alex Patel: Thanks @Sara. Sorry you're hitting this. Can you confirm whether the client is behind an ingress (nginx/ALB) and if there's any proxy buffering? Also whether they use gzip on response?

Sara Gomez: @Alex yes they're behind a corp nginx reverse proxy, and TLS is terminated at an edge. App dev says proxy has `proxy_buffering on` default.

Priya Menon: This looks like classic slow-consumer/backpressure. If the proxy buffers responses, the SSE semantics break — the proxy may hold chunks until buffer fills, and our runtime sees client slowness and triggers abort when write queues exceed threshold.

Miguel Ortiz: Can we get the exact nginx conf? Common culprits: `proxy_buffering on; proxy_buffer_size; proxy_max_temp_file_size` and `proxy_send_timeout`. Also `sendfile`/`tcp_nopush` settings can change behavior.

Sara Gomez: Customer sent config snippet: `proxy_buffering on; proxy_buffers 8 16k; proxy_busy_buffers_size 32k;`

Priya Menon: That explains it. If proxy buffers are smaller than event bursts, nginx will block reading from our connection and we see the socket backpressure. Two lines of remediation: 1) client/proxy change — disable buffering for SSE endpoints (`proxy_buffering off;`) and use `chunked_transfer_encoding on;` 2) server-side: make backpressure thresholds more visible, and provide a graceful slow-consumer mode instead of immediate abort.

Alex Patel: For the customer, immediate workaround: disable proxy buffering for the SSE route and ensure `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` passes through. Also suggest adding SSE keepalive: send `: keepalive\n\n` comments every 15s.

Karen Liu: Billing note — ticket INC-5631 flagged unexpected token counts after reconnects. If clients reconnect and the server replays partial tokens, they appear as new usage. We should track Last-Event-ID and id fields for SSE to allow de-dup on resume or recommend client-side id tracking.

Sara Gomez: They also provided a node repro that simulates a slow consumer. Attaching here: 
```js
// server.js (simple express SSE)
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.get('/sse', (req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream', 'Cache-Control': 'no-cache', Connection: 'keep-alive' });
  let i=0;
  const iv = setInterval(() => {
    const data = `event: message\\ndata: ${i}\\n\\n`;
    res.write(data); // if client is slow, this will back up
    i++;
    if (i>100) { clearInterval(iv); res.end(); }
  }, 100);
});
app.listen(3000);
```

Sara Gomez: And a slow client (reads 1 byte/sec) they used to trigger backpressure: 
```js
// slow-client.js
const http = require('http');
http.get('http://localhost:3000/sse', (res) => {
  res.on('data', (chunk) => {
    // artificially slow processing
    setTimeout(() => { process.stdout.write(chunk); }, 1000);
  });
});
```

Priya Menon: Nice repro. On our side, we see these error patterns: `write EAGAIN`, `EPIPE`, `socket hang up`, followed by `abort_stream(reason=slow_consumer_threshold)`. For Node-based runtime we can tune socket highWaterMark and call `socket.setNoDelay(true)` to reduce Nagle buffering. But that only helps marginally. Primary fix is to avoid intermediaries buffering SSE.

Alex Patel: Quick troubleshooting checklist for support to give customers: 
1) Confirm client path (direct vs behind proxy) and whether any L4/L7 product is buffering.
2) If behind nginx/traefik/ALB, set `proxy_buffering off` for SSE endpoints and forward chunked encoding.
3) Ensure client reads promptly; recommend backpressure-aware client libs (EventSource in browsers, or Node fetch with streaming reader).
4) Add SSE keepalives to prevent idle timeouts.
5) For billing concerns, recommend clients set `id:` fields per SSE event and use Last-Event-ID on reconnect to dedupe.

Miguel Ortiz: Also capture the following logs if they reoccur: `stream:slow-consumer`, `socket-writes-pending`, and the `write_queue_bytes` metric. Those are now captured in our infra metrics INF_METRICS.slow_consumer.* — include timestamps and request IDs when escalating.

Sara Gomez: Customer applied `proxy_buffering off;` and reported immediate improvement — stream stayed open and didn't abort. They still saw occasional partial event when they had a flaky Wi-Fi client.

Priya Menon: For flaky networks the resilient behavior is: 1) client should retry with Last-Event-ID and accept dedup; 2) runtime should implement a short grace window to allow slow consumers to catch up instead of aborting immediately; 3) track duplicate token issuance in billing to avoid charging duplicate tokens on immediate reconnects (billing dedupe window).

Karen Liu: Filing follow-up: billing team will investigate dedupe options. Assigning BL-2143 to look into short-term credit for affected customers and longer-term dedupe logic.

DeployBot: deploy status: patch/eng-runtime/slow-consumer-metrics deployed to staging at 2026-03-11T02:30:00Z (sha: a1b2c3d).

Priya Menon: Created internal bug INF-9876 to change abort behavior: add configurable slow_consumer_grace_ms (default 5000ms) and an opt-in `keep_partial_output` mode. Putting a canary behind a feature flag.

Alex Patel: Closing loop with customer: repro steps and config changes provided. Suggested they keep an eye on billing and forward any sample request_ids if they see token spikes. Thanks @Sara and @Miguel for the quick triage. :thumbsup:

Sara Gomez: Customer acknowledged, will provide request IDs if billing continues. Thanks team! :pray:
